<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tungabhadra River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tungabhadra is a river of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, formed by the junction of the Tunga and Bhadra rivers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, that flows north-east for about 530 km to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>. Vijayanagara (Hampi), the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, was built on its southern bank in the fourteenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur-doab">Raichur Doab</a> between the Tungabhadra and the Krishna was long contested by Vijayanagara and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river.jpg" alt="Tungabhadra River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hampi,_Tungabhadra_River,_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tughluq armies failed to recover the region, and by his death in 1358 Bahman Shah controlled a kingdom stretching from the Arabian Sea coast to the borders of Telangana, bounded in the south by the Krishna and <b>Tungabhadra</b> rivers, where it faced the newly founded Vijayanagara Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its early decades were spent fighting the neighbouring Deccan sultanates of Ahmadnagar, Golconda and Bidar, and the Vijayanagara Empire to the south over the fertile Raichur doab between the Krishna and <b>Tungabhadra</b> rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chalukya kingdom rested on the agriculture of the Deccan plateau and the valleys of the Krishna, Malaprabha and <b>Tungabhadra</b> rivers, where millets, pulses, cotton and, on irrigated land, rice were grown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The armies of Alauddin Khalji and Muhammad bin Tughluq had destroyed or humbled the old kingdoms of the Yadavas of Devagiri, the Kakatiyas of Warangal and the Pandyas of Madurai, and in 1327 annexed the small kingdom of Kampili on the <b>Tungabhadra River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Someshvara I, stricken by incurable illness in 1068, ended his life by drowning in the <b>Tungabhadra</b> river, a death recorded by the poet Bilhana and in inscriptions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Gadwal was established around 1663 by Pedda Soma Bhupal, a local chief who built the fort at Gadwal in the land between the Krishna and <b>Tungabhadra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gadwal-samasthan">Gadwal Samasthan</a></li></ul></section>
